## 0. Executive summary

The architecture is **genuinely good** — this is a well-layered, well-documented NestJS
codebase, well above average. Clean `config / common / infrastructure / features` split,
DI-token-based ports for swappable backends (`SEARCH_ENGINE`, `OBJECT_STORAGE`, `DRIZZLE`,
`REDIS_CLIENT`), a single error taxonomy with one envelope, fail-fast Zod env validation,
and a search-indexing pipeline (Postgres-as-truth → outbox → BullMQ → Meili, with
reconciliation + purge sweeps + admin status + health-integrated lag detection) that is
production-grade in design.

The problems are concentrated in **three places**:

1. **Authorization is not modelled per-tenant.** The RBAC layer only distinguishes
   `admin` vs "any authenticated". Several stores hold *per-user* data (uploaded document
   text, inbound email bodies, agent conversations), and the read paths over those stores
   are not owner-scoped. This is the highest-severity cluster (§B1, §B2, §C1, §C2).
2. **The mailbox module's IMAP integration is structurally wrong** — one TCP
   connect+login+logout per message, an `accountId` partition key that nothing honours,
   and flags that never round-trip (§B4).
3. **Dead / half-wired code and unused config** — `SessionCacheService`, `ExampleProcessor`,
   `MASTRA_APPROVAL_TTL_MS`, `MAILBOX_PUSH_FLAGS`, `ensureBucket()`, `deleteExpired()`,
   scheduled-report delivery. Several of these are the *missing half* of a feature that
   looks complete from the outside (§B5, §D).

Counts: **6 critical · 14 high · 21 medium · 18 low** (59 issues).

