# PART A — Architecture Review

## A.0 What is right (so it does not get "fixed")

- **Layering.** `common → config → infrastructure → features`, with `features` never
  imported by `infrastructure` except the one documented, `@Optional()`-guarded exception
  (`HealthModule → SearchServiceModule`, `health.module.ts`). Dependency direction is
  clean and the one violation is deliberate and justified in a comment.
- **Ports & adapters.** `SearchEngine`, `ObjectStorage`, `DrizzleDB` are interfaces behind
  symbols; MeiliSearch / MinIO / pg specifics never leak past their adapter file.
- **Error model.** One `AppException`, one `ERROR_REGISTRY` (code → status + kind +
  message), one `ErrorEnvelope`, one global filter. `INTERNAL` errors are message- and
  detail-stripped before rendering — correct, and rare to see done properly.
- **Config.** Zod schema validating **all** env at boot with production-only `superRefine`
  guards that reject dev-default JWT secrets, MinIO creds, all-zero encryption keys, and
  missing Meili master keys. Genuinely strong.
- **Search pipeline.** Transactional batch upsert, checksum-based no-op skip, partial
  indexes matching sweep predicates exactly, exponential per-record backoff, race-repair
  after a reload (`redriveReloadRacers`), `updatedAt` deliberately protected from index
  stamps so `updatedAt > indexedAt` stays a truthful drift predicate. Excellent.
- **Documentation.** Comments explain *why*, cite installed type definitions by path and
  line, and record rejected alternatives. This is the strongest part of the codebase.

## A.1 Architectural issues

### ~~A-1~~ ✅ FIXED · CRITICAL · The authorization model has only two tiers, but the data is multi-tenant
`src/common/guards/roles.guard.ts`, `src/common/principal.ts`

`RolesGuard` answers exactly one question: "is the caller's role in the `@Roles(...)` set?"
Routes with no `@Roles` are open to **every authenticated principal**. There is no notion
of resource ownership anywhere in the guard/decorator layer — ownership is re-implemented
ad hoc inside three services (`FileService.loadOwned`, `ConversationService.getOwned`,
`MailboxRepository` scoping), inconsistently, and **not at all** in the search feature.

Because the search-service is a *generic* store that other features write per-user data
into (`documents.ownerUserId`, `inbound_email.bodyText`), "reads are global" (the documented
policy in `search-record.service.ts`) silently promotes every writer's data to
all-authenticated-users visibility. See B1/B2 for the concrete exploits.

**Fix direction:** introduce a per-collection visibility policy (`public` | `owner-scoped`
| `admin-only`) on the `collections` row, and have `SearchRecordService.search/get` inject
a mandatory `ownerField = principal.id` filter for owner-scoped collections. Do it in the
service, not the controller, so the agent tool path is covered by the same rule.

### ~~A-2~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · Access tokens are unrevocable, and the revocation infrastructure that exists is dead
`src/features/auth/strategies/jwt.strategy.ts:23-25`, `src/infrastructure/cache/session/session-cache.service.ts`

`JwtStrategy.validate` returns `{ id: payload.sub, role: payload.role }` from the token
alone — no DB check, no denylist. Consequences, all within the 900s access TTL:

- `POST /auth/logout-all` and `PATCH /auth/password` revoke *refresh* sessions only; the
  attacker's existing access token keeps working.
- A soft-deleted user (`DELETE /users/:id`) keeps full API access.
- A revoked service credential (`DELETE /service-credentials/:id`) keeps full API access.
- A role change (`PATCH /users/:id {role}`) does not take effect until the token expires;
  a demoted admin stays admin.

`SessionCacheService` — a Redis-backed session store that would solve exactly this — is
provided, exported, wired into `AppModule`, and **has zero call sites in the entire repo**
(verified by grep). The module is pure overhead today.

**Fix direction:** either add a `jti` + Redis denylist checked in `JwtStrategy.validate`,
or add a cheap `users.updatedAt`/`token_version` check. Then delete `SessionCacheModule`
or actually use it.

### ~~A-3~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · Throttling is per-process, so rate limits evaporate on a multi-replica deploy
`src/app.module.ts:40-47`

`ThrottlerModule.forRootAsync` is configured with a TTL/limit but **no storage provider**,
so it falls back to `ThrottlerStorageService` (in-memory `Map`). With N replicas the
effective limit is `N × THROTTLE_LIMIT`, and it resets on every deploy. This matters most
for the endpoints that deliberately carry tight limits: `POST /auth/login` (5/min),
`POST /auth/forgot-password` (3/15min), `POST /auth/service-token` (10/min) — i.e. exactly
the credential-brute-force surface.

Redis is already a hard dependency and `REDIS_CLIENT` is globally exported; wiring
`@nest-lab/throttler-storage-redis` (or an equivalent) is a small change.

### ~~A-4~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `validateEnv(process.env)` runs 11× at boot, once per config namespace
`src/config/configurations/*.config.ts` (every file), `src/config/config.module.ts:29`

Each `registerAs` factory calls `validateEnv(process.env)` and re-parses the *entire*
schema, in addition to the `validate:` hook. Eleven full Zod parses + eleven
`superRefine` passes. Harmless for correctness (the function is pure) but it is duplicated
work and, more importantly, means the namespaced configs read `process.env` directly rather
than the already-validated object — so `ConfigModule`'s `expandVariables: true` expansion
is not applied to them.

**Fix:** memoise (`let cached: Env | undefined`) or have `validate` stash the parsed result
and let the factories read from it.

### ~~A-5~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `main.ts` bypasses validated config for the listen port
`src/main.ts:32`

`const port = Number(process.env.PORT ?? 3000)` — raw env, while `appConfig.port` is the
validated, coerced value. Two sources of truth for the same setting; a `PORT=abc` would
`listen(NaN)` here despite the schema rejecting it in the health of every other consumer.

### A-6 ⏸ DEFERRED · MEDIUM · No global route prefix or API versioning
`src/main.ts`

`setGlobalPrefix` / `enableVersioning` are never called. Routes live at the root
(`/auth`, `/users`, `/files`, `/search/...`), while the Mastra adapter mounts its catch-all
at `/api/agent-core`. The API surface is therefore inconsistent, and there is no
forward path for a breaking change to a public endpoint.

### ~~A-7~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · Postgres pool is unsized while the app runs 5 BullMQ workers + 5 schedulers
`src/infrastructure/database/database.module.ts:23-31`

`new Pool({host, port, user, password, database, ssl})` — no `max`,
`idleTimeoutMillis`, `connectionTimeoutMillis`, or `statement_timeout`. The pg default is
`max: 10` for the **whole process**, and that pool is shared by: HTTP request handlers,
5 queue workers (`search-indexing` at `SEARCH_INDEX_CONCURRENCY=4`, `file-processing`,
`document-ingest`, `mailbox-sync`, `agent-run`), 5 bootstrap schedulers, **and** Mastra's
`PostgresStore` (agent memory + workflow snapshots, `memory.factory.ts:19`). Under load
the search indexer alone can hold 4 of the 10 connections while `waitForTask` parks it.
Expect pool starvation presenting as request latency, not as an error.

Also: `connectionTimeoutMillis` unset means a request waits on the pool **forever**.

### ~~A-8~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `ssl: { rejectUnauthorized: false }` disables TLS certificate validation
`src/infrastructure/database/database.module.ts:29`, `src/infrastructure/database/seeders/seed.ts:24`

When `DATABASE_SSL=true` — i.e. exactly when someone is trying to *secure* the DB link —
the certificate is not verified, so the connection is encrypted but not authenticated
(MITM-able). Should be configurable (`DATABASE_SSL_CA` / `DATABASE_SSL_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED`)
and default to `true` in production.

### ~~A-9~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `/health` is fully public and leaks internal dependency detail
`src/infrastructure/health/health.controller.ts:19-21,38-44`

`@Public()` + no rate-limit exemption or auth. On failure the indicators return raw driver
messages (`indicator.down({ message: (error as Error).message })`) — e.g. Postgres
`ECONNREFUSED 10.0.x.y:5432`, MinIO bucket names, Meili errors — to any unauthenticated
caller. It also runs 4 live network round-trips per hit, making it a cheap amplification
target (the global throttler does apply, but see A-3).

Standard split: public `/health/live` (process only), authenticated-or-internal
`/health/ready` (dependencies, detail redacted).

### ~~A-10~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `AppModule` import order is load-bearing but unenforced
`src/app.module.ts:38-71`

Comments correctly note "MastraModule must remain last (catch-all controller)" and
"ObservabilityModule first". Nothing enforces either. A routine alphabetise-the-imports
refactor silently breaks routing for every module after Mastra. Consider mounting the
Mastra adapter behind an explicit, narrow prefix guard, or add a boot-time assertion.

### ~~A-11~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · `src/application/` is an empty directory
Leftover scaffolding; either use it or remove it.

### ~~A-12~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · CORS defaults to reflecting any origin
`src/main.ts:22-26`

`origin: auth.corsOrigins.length > 0 ? auth.corsOrigins : true` — with `CORS_ORIGINS`
unset (its schema default is `''`), every origin is allowed. Risk is limited because
`credentials: false` and the app is Bearer-only, but the *default* should be restrictive
and the production `superRefine` block should require `CORS_ORIGINS` to be non-empty,
the way it already does for `JWT_ACCESS_SECRET` and friends.

### ~~A-13~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · No request/JSON body size limit configured; DTO limits contradict the transport limit
`src/main.ts`, `src/features/search-service/dto/persist-records.dto.ts:8-16`

No `bodyParser` config → Express's 100 kB JSON default. But `persistRecordsSchema` accepts
up to **1000 records** of arbitrary `z.record(z.unknown())`. Any realistic batch is
rejected with a bare `PayloadTooLargeError` (mapped to a generic 413/500) before validation
runs, so the documented API contract is unreachable. Pick one limit and make both agree.

