# PART B — Per-module analysis

## B1. search-service — CRITICAL cross-tenant read exposure

### ~~B1-1~~ ✅ FIXED · CRITICAL · Any authenticated user can read every record in every collection
`src/features/search-service/search.controller.ts:18` (`POST /search/collections/:name/query`)
`src/features/search-service/search.controller.ts:25-26` (`GET /search/collections/:name/records/:id`)

`SearchQueryController` carries **no `@Roles`** at class or method level (only `reload` is
admin-gated at line 32). `SearchRecordService.search()` applies no principal filter — the
only filters are the ones the *caller* supplies, validated against the collection's
filterable allowlist.

Two collections are auto-created by the system and hold per-user / confidential data:

| Collection | Created by | Contents |
|---|---|---|
| `documents` | `documents-collection.bootstrap.ts` | full extracted text of **every user's** uploaded PDF/DOCX/MD, plus `ownerUserId` |
| `inbound_email` | `mailbox.service.ts:57-73` | `subject`, **full `bodyText`**, `fromAddress`, `threadId` of every ingested message |

So a plain `role: 'user'` account can run:

```
POST /search/collections/inbound_email/query   { "q": "", "limit": 100 }
POST /search/collections/documents/query       { "q": "password", "limit": 100 }
```

and receive full email bodies and other users' document text. `attributesToRetrieve` is
never restricted, so hits are returned whole. `GET .../records/:id` additionally reads the
row straight from **Postgres** and returns `document` in full, resolvable by `externalId`
— which for `documents` is the `fileId`, and for `inbound_email` is the message UUID.

Note the mailbox REST API is correctly `@Roles('admin')` (`mailbox.controller.ts:20`) —
the search endpoint is a complete bypass of that gate.

This is not accidental drift: `test/search.e2e-spec.ts:310` asserts a non-admin
`asUser(...)` query **succeeds**. The policy is deliberate; it is the policy that is wrong
once system collections exist.

**Fix:** per-collection visibility (see A-1). Minimum viable stopgap: mark `documents` and
`inbound_email` non-queryable via the generic endpoint, and force an `ownerUserId` filter
for the former.

### ~~B1-2~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · Collection schemas are readable by any authenticated user
`src/features/search-service/collection.controller.ts:33-44`

`GET /search/collections` and `GET /search/collections/:name` have no `@Roles` (only
create/update/delete do). Any user can enumerate every collection and its full field spec,
which is the reconnaissance step for B1-1 and reveals internal data modelling.

### ~~B1-3~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · `indexAttempts` is incremented on **success**, poisoning backoff and stuck-detection
`src/features/search-service/search-record.repository.ts:161`

```ts
indexAttempts: sql`${searchRecords.indexAttempts} + 1`,
```
in `markIndexStateMany`, which is called for `'INDEXED'` (success) as well as `'FAILED'`.
A record has no way to reset the counter except a fresh `persist` upsert (line 132,
`excluded.index_attempts`). Records that are only ever *re-indexed* — every record touched
by `POST .../reload`, or by `reconcileCollection`, or re-driven by `redriveReloadRacers` —
accumulate attempts monotonically.

Two concrete consequences:
- `findUnsynced`'s backoff is `staleMs × 2^least(indexAttempts,20)`. After ~10 reloads a
  record that *then* fails once is immediately parked at `SEARCH_RECONCILE_MAX_BACKOFF_MS`
  (1h default) instead of retrying in 5 minutes.
- `reconcile()` logs `"N record(s) have failed >= SEARCH_MAX_INDEX_ATTEMPTS and need
  operator attention"` for records that have never failed. The stuck-record alarm — the
  main operational signal this pipeline exposes — produces false positives by design.

**Fix:** increment only on the `'FAILED'` path; reset to 0 on `'INDEXED'`.

### ~~B1-4~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · A collection whose index-drop fails permanently traps its records in a retry loop
`search-indexing.processor.ts:302-309` (`dropIndex`), `collection.service.ts:711-729` (`remove`)

`remove()` soft-deletes the rows `PENDING`, then tries `deleteIndex` + `waitForTask`. On
failure it enqueues `DROP_INDEX_JOB`. But if the index is *already gone* (double-delete,
manual Meili cleanup, wiped volume), Meili's delete task fails with `index_not_found`,
`waitForTask` throws, BullMQ exhausts 3 attempts, and the job dies. The records stay
`PENDING` forever.

Then the reconciliation sweep picks them up (`findUnsynced` ignores collection liveness),
routes them to `indexRecords`, where `ensureIndexReady` returns early (`registry.resolve`
is `null` for a deleted collection) and `deleteDocuments` runs against a non-existent
index → task fails → rows marked `FAILED` → re-swept forever. They can also never be
purged, because `purgeSoftDeleted` requires `indexState = 'INDEXED'`.

**Fix:** treat `index_not_found` as success in `dropIndex` (and in `indexRecords`' delete
branch) — the desired end state has been reached.

### ~~B1-5~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `POST .../reload` blanks the collection for the duration of the rebuild
`search-indexing.processor.ts:185-186`

`clearIndex` + `waitForTask`, *then* repage from Postgres. For a large collection every
query returns zero hits for the whole window. The `redriveReloadRacers` mechanism handles
correctness afterwards, but availability is not addressed. The standard fix is
build-into-`{name}_next` then `swapIndexes`, which Meili supports natively.

### ~~B1-6~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `needsEnsure` only checks coverage, so narrowing a field spec never converges
`src/infrastructure/search-engine/search-engine.service.ts:83-96` + `covers()` at the file tail

`covers(actual, required)` returns true when `actual ⊇ required`. Removing `filterable`
from a field therefore leaves the attribute filterable in Meili indefinitely — the settings
drift is invisible to `needsEnsure`, so `ensureIndex` is never re-run for it. `update()`
does call `ensureIndex` directly on a field change so the immediate path is fine, but any
convergence *after* that (new process, index recreated from defaults) reverts silently.
Documented as intentional ("a superset passes"); the consequence — a removed filter still
being usable — is a policy leak, not just cosmetics.

### ~~B1-7~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · Sort clause parsing accepts malformed input silently
`search-record.service.ts` `buildSort`

`entry.split(':')` on `"a:b:c"` yields `field='a', dir='b'` and the rest is dropped. It
fails closed (`dir` must be `asc|desc`), so this is a robustness nit rather than an
injection — but the filter/sort/facet builders are the security boundary against Meili
filter injection, and they deserve exact-shape validation, not prefix parsing.
(The `quote()` escaping for string filter values **is** correct.)

### ~~B1-8~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · `findUnsyncedByCollection` doc comment contradicts its `ORDER BY`
`search-record.repository.ts`

Comment says "Most-recently-attempted unconverged records"; the query orders by
`indexAttempts DESC`. The behaviour (worst offenders first) matches the caller's intent —
fix the comment.

### ~~B1-9~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · `errorCode()`'s JSDoc block is attached to `covers()`
`src/infrastructure/search-engine/search-engine.service.ts` (file tail)

A long, valuable comment about reading `error.cause.code` sits directly above `covers()`,
whose own doc comment follows it. Misleading on read.

### ~~B1-10~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · `SEARCH_MAX_TOTAL_HITS` is index-level but validated against page size only
`env.validation.ts` superRefine

The guard (`maxTotalHits >= maxPageSize`) is correct but weak; a user paging past
`maxTotalHits / limit` gets silently empty pages with a saturated `totalHits`. Worth
returning an explicit error or clamping `page` in `SearchRecordService.search`.

## B2. file-processor — CRITICAL dedup crosses ownership and deletion boundaries

### ~~B2-1~~ ✅ FIXED · CRITICAL · Checksum dedup ignores both `ownerId` and `isDeleted`
`src/features/file-processor/file.repository.ts:22-32`, used at `file.service.ts:93` and `236`

```ts
.where(and(eq(files.checksumSha256, checksum), eq(files.status, 'AVAILABLE')))
```

No `ownerId` predicate, no `isDeleted = false` predicate. `tryDedup` then creates a **new
row owned by the caller** pointing at the existing `objectKey`. Two distinct failures:

1. **Cross-owner content access.** A caller who knows (or can obtain) the SHA-256 of
   another user's file gets a fully-owned file row for it and can immediately
   `GET /files/:id/download-url`. The presigned URL is issued because `loadOwned` passes —
   the *new* row is legitimately theirs. Realistic for any content whose hash is knowable:
   a shared/leaked document, a standard form, a template, or anything the attacker has
   previously seen.
2. **Existence oracle.** `initiateUpload` returns `{deduplicated: true}` vs a presigned
   upload target, so any SHA-256 can be probed for presence in the system without
   uploading a byte.
3. **Soft-delete resurrection.** A user "deletes" a file (soft-delete only). Its row is
   still `AVAILABLE` (delete does not change `status`), so the very next dedup hit revives
   the content under a new owner — and the object is still in MinIO until the hourly
   reconcile sweep runs.

**Fix:** scope the lookup to `ownerId` **and** `isDeleted = false`, or drop cross-row dedup
entirely and let MinIO/content-addressing handle physical dedup with an explicit refcount.

### ~~B2-2~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · Soft-deleted files are hard-deleted and their bytes purged on the next sweep
`file.repository.ts:72-78` (`findPurgeable`), `file-processing.processor.ts` `reconcile()`

```ts
.where(eq(files.isDeleted, true)).limit(limit)
```
No age cutoff. The reconciliation sweep runs hourly (`FILE_RECONCILE_EVERY_MS = 3_600_000`)
and, for every soft-deleted row, removes the object then `hardDelete`s the row. So
"soft delete" has a ≤1h retention and is irrecoverable afterwards — no undo, no audit
trail, and the DB row that would explain what happened is gone too.

Contrast the search-service, which does this properly (`SEARCH_PURGE_AFTER_DAYS = 30` +
a confirmed-converged gate). Add a `FILE_PURGE_AFTER_DAYS` equivalent.

### ~~B2-3~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · Purge/dedup race can delete bytes that a live row still references
`file-processing.processor.ts` `reconcile()` vs `file.service.ts:309` `tryDedup`

`reconcile` checks `countLiveReferences(objectKey) === 0` then `removeObject`. A concurrent
`tryDedup` can insert a new live row against that same `objectKey` between the check and
the delete (no lock, no transaction spanning both). Result: a live, `AVAILABLE` file row
whose object is gone — `GET /files/:id/download-url` returns a presigned URL that 404s.

### ~~B2-4~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `?ttl` on the download URL is unvalidated and unbounded
`src/features/file-processor/file.controller.ts:56-63`

Raw `@Query('ttl')`, hand-parsed, not a Zod DTO. Any positive finite number is forwarded to
`presignedGetUrl`. MinIO rejects > 604800s, so the practical outcome is a 500 rather than a
7-year URL — but a caller can still mint a **7-day** presigned link for any file they own,
regardless of `MINIO_PRESIGN_EXPIRY` (default 300s). The whole point of a short presign TTL
is that the URL is a bearer token; letting the client choose its lifetime defeats it.
Clamp to `min(requested, presignExpirySeconds)` and move to a DTO.

### ~~B2-5~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · Files are `AVAILABLE` and downloadable before integrity verification
`file.service.ts` `completeUpload` → `enqueueProcessing`

`completeUpload` trusts the client-supplied `sha256` (`dto?.sha256 ?? row.checksumSha256`),
flips the row to `AVAILABLE`, and *then* queues verification. Between those two points the
file is downloadable and — worse — **dedupable by other users** (B2-1). An attacker can
declare a victim's checksum on garbage content, then race a dedup lookup before the
processor quarantines it. Documented as async-by-design, but the dedup interaction is not.

### ~~B2-6~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · Magic-byte MIME sniffing covers 5 signatures
`file.util.ts` `detectMimeFromMagic`

PDF/PNG/GIF/ZIP/JPEG only, and only the first 16 bytes are captured
(`file-processing.processor.ts`, `head.length < 16`). Everything else returns `null` =
"not a mismatch", so `text/plain`-declared HTML/SVG/JS sails through. The code itself
suggests swapping in `file-type`; worth doing, since the quarantine gate is the only
content check before `document-ingest` parses the bytes.

### ~~B2-7~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · No admin override path for file access
`file.service.ts:300-307` (`loadOwned`)

`row.ownerId !== owner.id` → 404, with no role check. An `admin` cannot inspect or
moderate a user's file at all, and there is no support/abuse workflow. Meanwhile
`SYSTEM_PRINCIPAL` (`id: null`) matches *all* rows with `ownerId IS NULL` — including
every mailbox raw `.eml` and attachment — so the system principal is an implicit
shared bucket rather than a scoped identity.

### ~~B2-8~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · `Content-Disposition` filename sanitisation strips only `"`
`src/infrastructure/file-manage/object-storage.service.ts:85`

`opts.downloadFilename.replace(/"/g, '')` — CR/LF, `;`, and non-ASCII pass through into
`response-content-disposition`. The MinIO SDK URL-encodes it into the query string, so this
is not exploitable today, but the sanitiser reads as if it were the defence. Use RFC 5987
`filename*=UTF-8''...` encoding, or a strict allowlist.

### ~~B2-9~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · Stale doc comment claims there is no auth guard
`file.controller.ts:24-27`

"A real auth guard binds here later (`@UseGuards(AuthGuard)`); today the principal comes
