## B3. auth

### ~~B3-1~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · Login is a user-enumeration timing oracle
`src/features/auth/auth.service.ts:54-66`

```ts
const user = await this.users.findByEmail(email.toLowerCase());
const ok = user ? await this.passwords.verify(user.passwordHash, password) : false;
```
When the user does not exist, `argon2.verify` is skipped entirely. Argon2id is deliberately
slow (tens to hundreds of ms), so "unknown email" responses are an order of magnitude
faster than "wrong password" ones — trivially measurable over the network.

The same codebase gets this *right* in `PasswordResetService.reset`, which verifies against
`DECOY_CODE_HASH` "to equalize HMAC timing" (`password-reset.service.ts:78,86`). Apply the
same pattern: verify against a precomputed dummy argon2 hash on the not-found path.

### ~~B3-2~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · Concurrent refresh requests both succeed (no rotation atomicity)
`auth.service.ts` `refresh()`

`findByTokenHash` → check `revokedAt` → `revokeById` → issue new pair. Two concurrent
requests with the same refresh token both read `revokedAt = null` and both mint a pair,
splitting the family into two live lineages. The theft-detection design (`revokeFamily` on
replay) is undermined: a stolen token racing the legitimate client goes undetected.
Use a conditional update (`UPDATE ... WHERE id = ? AND revoked_at IS NULL RETURNING`) and
treat "0 rows" as reuse.

### ~~B3-3~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `sessions` and `password_reset_codes` grow without bound
`session.repository.ts`, `password-reset.repository.ts:72`

There is no cleanup job for expired/revoked sessions. `PasswordResetRepository.deleteExpired`
exists and is unit-tested, but **has no production call site** (verified by grep) — no
scheduler, no processor. Both tables accumulate forever; `sessions` gets a row per login
per device, permanently.

### ~~B3-4~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `service_credentials` lookups ignore `isDeleted`
`service-credential.repository.ts` `findByKeyHash` / `list`

`findByKeyHash` filters on nothing but the hash (revocation is checked in the service via
`revokedAt`, which is fine), but `list()` returns soft-deleted credentials too. Given
`serviceCredentials` uses `baseColumns`, `isDeleted = false` should be in both.

### ~~B3-5~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · Service-credential principals are structurally incompatible with user-FK columns
`service-credential.service.ts` `exchangeForToken` (`sub: cred.id`)

The issued token's `sub` is a `service_credentials.id`, but downstream tables treat
`principal.id` as a `users.id` with a real foreign key. See C-2 — this is an integration
bug, filed there.

### ~~B3-6~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · `changePassword`/`logoutAll`/`listSessions` cast `user.id as string`
`auth.controller.ts:73,86,96`

`Principal.id` is `string | null`. A service-credential token reaches these handlers with a
non-null id that is not a user id (→ 401 from `findActiveById`, fine) but the cast is
load-bearing and unchecked. A future `@Public()` route reaching these would pass `null` into
a SQL `eq()`.

### ~~B3-7~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · Reset codes are 6 digits with 5 attempts and no per-account lockout window
`auth.config.ts:24-28`

Sound as configured, but the values are hard-coded in the config factory rather than
env-driven, unlike every other tunable in the file.

### ~~B3-8~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · No `audience` claim on access tokens
`token.service.ts` / `jwt.strategy.ts`

`issuer` is set and verified; `audience` is not. Fine for a single-service deployment, worth
adding before a second service shares the secret.

## B4. mailbox — the IMAP integration is structurally wrong

### ~~B4-1~~ ✅ FIXED · CRITICAL · One full IMAP connect + login + logout **per message**
`src/infrastructure/email/transport/imap.transport.ts` (`withClient` wraps every exported fn)
`src/features/mailbox/mailbox-ingest.service.ts:62-77`

`withClient` opens a new `ImapFlow`, `connect()`s, runs one operation, then `logout()`s.
`MailboxIngestService.sync` then does:

```
mailboxState()      → connection #1
listUidsSince()     → connection #2
for (const uid of uids)          // up to MAILBOX_BATCH_CAP = 200
    fetchForIngest(uid)          → connections #3 … #202
```

With the default batch cap that is **up to 202 TCP connections + TLS handshakes + IMAP
logins per sync**, and the poll runs every 5 minutes. Gmail caps ~15 simultaneous IMAP
connections and rate-limits login attempts; most providers will throttle or temporarily
lock the account. Even against a permissive server this is ~200× the necessary latency and
server load.

**Fix:** add a session-scoped API (`withSession(conn, fn)`) that opens one connection and
one mailbox lock, and rewrite `sync()` to fetch the whole UID range in a single streaming
`client.fetch(uids, {...}, {uid:true})`.

### ~~B4-2~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · `accountId` is a fictional partition key — every "account" reads the same mailbox
`mailbox.service.ts:75-84` (`resolveAccountId`), `inbox.service.ts:15-19` (`conn()`)

The API accepts an arbitrary `accountId` UUID (`SyncDto`, `ListMessagesDto`) and partitions
`email_messages` / `email_sync_state` by it. But `InboxService.conn()` **ignores it
entirely** and always resolves the single `imap_configs` row where `isActive = true`.
`MAILBOX_DEFAULT_ACCOUNT_ID` is a free-form env UUID with no relationship to any
`imap_configs.id`.

So: syncing as `accountId=A` and `accountId=B` pulls from the *same* mailbox and writes
two independent copies with independent UID cursors. The schema promises multi-account;
the transport delivers one. Either make `accountId` the `imap_configs.id` and resolve the
connection from it, or delete the column and the DTO fields.

### ~~B4-3~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · `receivedAt` is set from the *sent* date, not the server's INTERNALDATE
`mailbox-ingest.service.ts:204`

```ts
receivedAt: msg.sentAt ?? new Date(),
```
`sentAt` is the `Date:` header — attacker/sender-controlled and semantically different.
`receivedAt` is the primary list ordering (`email_messages_list_idx`,
`listMessages ORDER BY receivedAt DESC`) *and* the reconciliation window anchor. A message
with a back-dated or forged `Date:` header sorts to the bottom of the inbox and can fall
outside the 7-day reconcile lookback. IMAP INTERNALDATE is available from `fetchOne`
(`internalDate`) and is the correct source.

### ~~B4-4~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · Message flags never round-trip; `MAILBOX_PUSH_FLAGS` is dead config
`mailbox.service.ts` `markSeen`, `mailbox.config.ts:12`

- `PATCH /mailbox/messages/:id/seen` updates the local row and re-indexes, but never calls
  `InboxService.markSeen` — the IMAP server is never told. Read state diverges immediately
  and permanently.
- `seen` is captured only at first ingest (`sync` skips messages already in the DB), so a
  message marked read in a real mail client stays unread forever in this system.
- `MAILBOX_PUSH_FLAGS` exists in the config namespace and its spec, and is referenced by
  **nothing** (verified by grep) — it is the switch for the feature that was never wired.
- `setSeen` exists in `imap.transport.ts` and has no production caller.

### ~~B4-5~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `email_messages.uid` is a signed 32-bit `integer`; IMAP UIDs are unsigned 32-bit
`src/infrastructure/database/schema/mailbox.schema.ts`

`integer('uid')` caps at 2,147,483,647. IMAP UIDs go to 4,294,967,295 and long-lived
mailboxes do exceed 2^31. Beyond that, inserts fail with a numeric-overflow error. Use
`bigint` (as `uidValidity` already correctly does).

### ~~B4-6~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `upsertSyncState` is read-then-write and races itself
`mailbox.repository.ts` `upsertSyncState`

`getSyncState` → branch → insert/update. Two concurrent syncs for the same
`(accountId, mailbox)` both see "no row" and both insert, violating
`email_sync_state_account_mailbox_idx` → 500. Use a real `onConflictDoUpdate` against the
partial unique index (the pattern already used in `SearchRecordRepository.upsertMany`).

### ~~B4-7~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · No referential integrity across the mailbox tables
`mailbox.schema.ts`

`emailMessages.accountId`, `emailMessages.rawFileId`, `emailAttachments.emailId`, and
`emailAttachments.fileId` are all bare `uuid` columns with **no `references()`**. Every
other schema module in the repo declares its FKs (`agent.schema.ts`, `identity.schema.ts`,
`system.schema.ts`, `password-reset.schema.ts`). Attachments can outlive their message; a
`rawFileId` can point at a hard-deleted `files` row (very likely, given B2-2).

### ~~B4-8~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · Attachments are uploaded to MinIO before the message row is inserted
`mailbox-ingest.service.ts` `persist()`

`putFromStream` for the raw `.eml` and each attachment runs first, creating `files` rows;
`insertMessageWithAttachments` runs after. If the insert throws, the `files` rows are
orphaned (owned by `SYSTEM_PRINCIPAL`, referenced by nothing) and will be reaped by the
file sweep — but only after the object bytes were written and paid for. On retry the
attachments are uploaded again (dedup helps only when checksums are computed, which they
are for Buffers, so this is mostly benign — but the raw `.eml` filename is `${uid}.eml`
which will collide across mailboxes).

### ~~B4-9~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · `findByIdWithAttachments` does not filter soft-deleted attachments
`mailbox.repository.ts`

The message is checked (`message.isDeleted`), the attachment query is not.

### ~~B4-10~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · Reconciliation is hard-coded to the single default account
`schedulers/mailbox-reconciliation.scheduler.ts`

Only `MAILBOX_DEFAULT_ACCOUNT_ID` + `MAILBOX_DEFAULT_MAILBOX` are swept. Consistent with
B4-2 (there is really only one account) but inconsistent with the API surface.

## B5. mastra (AI agent)

### ~~B5-1~~ ✅ FIXED · CRITICAL · Conversations with a NULL owner are readable and resumable by anyone
`src/features/mastra/services/conversation.service.ts:75` and `:119`

```ts
if (principal.id && existing.ownerUserId && existing.ownerUserId !== principal.id)
```
The check short-circuits when `ownerUserId` is `NULL`. `agentConversations.ownerUserId` is
nullable and is set to `principal.id` — which is `null` for `SYSTEM_PRINCIPAL`, used by
every scheduled/system-triggered run (`agent-run.processor.ts`). So **any** authenticated
user can:

- `GET /agent/conversations/:id/messages` on a system conversation and read its full
  transcript (`conversation-messages.service.ts` → `getOwned` → same hole), and
- `POST /agent/chat {conversationId}` to continue it, injecting into a system thread.

### ~~B5-2~~ ✅ FIXED · CRITICAL · The same hole lets any user approve another principal's side-effecting tool call
`src/features/mastra/services/approval.service.ts` `decide()`, `chat-stream.service.ts` `resume()`

Both gate on `principal.role !== 'admin'` → `conversations.getOwned(principal, appr.conversationId)`.
With B5-1, a NULL-owner conversation passes that check for everybody. `decide()` then calls
`resumeAfterApproval`, which executes the pending tool — `send-email` or `db-write`, both
declared `requireApproval: true` precisely because they are real, external side-effects.

Net effect: the human-in-the-loop gate, the module's central safety control, is bypassable
by any authenticated user for any system-initiated run. Fix B5-1 (treat NULL owner as
"system-only, admin-only") and this closes with it.

### ~~B5-3~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · The agent's `search-query` tool has no tenancy scoping
`src/features/mastra/tools/search-query.tool.ts:28-48`

`searchDocumentsExecute` correctly injects `filters: { ownerUserId: rt.principal.id }` and
returns `EMPTY` for an anonymous principal — a good pattern. `searchQueryExecute` does
none of that: it blocks only the literal string `'documents'` and forwards
`{collection, query, filters}` straight through. So any user can prompt the agent to
`search-query` the `inbound_email` collection and receive email bodies. The `documents`
block is also trivially defeated by any future alias/second document collection.

This is the confused-deputy form of B1-1 and needs the same collection-visibility fix.

### ~~B5-4~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · Scheduled reports are never delivered
`workflows/scheduled-report.workflow.ts:78-85`, `processors/agent-run.processor.ts`

The `deliver` step:
- `deliveryChannel: 'email'` → `suspend({reason: 'external email requires approval'})`.
  The processor's `'suspended'` branch explicitly logs that resuming a scheduled workflow
  is "deferred to v2" — so nothing ever resumes it. The email is never sent.
- `deliveryChannel: 'conversation'` (the **default**) → `return { delivered: true }` and
  nothing else. The comment says "the processor persists the report into the conversation",
  but `AgentRunProcessor` never touches a conversation — it creates the run with no
  `conversationId` and only stamps status.

So the whole scheduled-report feature produces a `agent_run.output` blob and no user-visible
output, on either channel, while reporting `status: 'succeeded'`. `agentSchedules.deliveryTarget`
is likewise never read.

### ~~B5-5~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · The `MASTRA_SCHEDULES_ENABLED` kill-switch does not disable schedule creation
`schedulers/agent-schedule.scheduler.ts` vs `services/schedule.service.ts:74`

`AgentScheduleScheduler.onApplicationBootstrap` honours the flag and skips boot-time
registration. But `ScheduleService.create` calls `if (row.enabled) await this.register(row)`
unconditionally — so `POST /agent/schedules` registers a live BullMQ repeatable even when
schedules are supposed to be off. The switch only survives until the first admin creates a
schedule, and then only until the next restart.

### ~~B5-6~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · Approvals never expire; `MASTRA_APPROVAL_TTL_MS` is dead config
`mastra.config.ts:12`, `agent.schema.ts` (`agentApprovals.expiresAt`), `approval.repository.ts`

`approvalTtlMs` is validated, namespaced, and read by nothing (verified by grep).
`expiresAt` is declared on the table and never populated by any `create` call. The
`approvalStatus` enum has an `'expired'` member that no code path can reach. Pending
approvals — each one a *suspended Mastra run holding a workflow snapshot in Postgres* —
accumulate indefinitely and remain approvable months later.

### ~~B5-7~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `GET /agent/conversations` breaks if Mastra's tables do not exist yet
`repositories/conversation.repository.ts:59-66`

`listByOwner` `LEFT JOIN`s `mastra.mastra_threads`, a table created and owned by
`@mastra/pg` on first use — not by any Drizzle migration (correctly excluded from the
barrel, per the comment). On a freshly migrated database, before any agent run,
`mastra.mastra_threads` may not exist and this query throws `relation does not exist` →
500 on the conversation list. Needs a `to_regclass` guard, a bootstrap
`store.init()`, or an explicit migration that creates the schema.

### ~~B5-8~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · Agent runs have no timeout and hold an HTTP request open
`agent-runner.service.ts` `runChat`, `chat.controller.ts` `chat`

`agent.generate(...)` is awaited with no `AbortSignal`/deadline. A slow or hung model call
occupies a Node request slot indefinitely; `MASTRA_MAX_RETRIES` (default 2) multiplies it.
The SSE path is better (the client sees progress) but also has no server-side cap, and
`res.write` return values are ignored so there is no backpressure handling.

### ~~B5-9~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · Admin cron expressions are not validated before registration
`dto/schedule.dto.ts` (`cron: z.string().min(1).max(120)`), `schedule.service.ts` `register`

Any string is accepted and handed to `upsertJobScheduler({pattern})`, which parses it with
`cron-parser` and throws. The throw surfaces from `create()` as an unmapped 500 *after* the
`agent_schedule` row has already been inserted — so a bad cron leaves a persisted schedule
that will be retried at every boot by `syncRepeatableJobs()` and fail there too.

### ~~B5-10~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `agentSchedules.agentId` is stored, exposed, and ignored
`schedule.dto.ts`, `agent-run.processor.ts`

The processor always runs `SCHEDULED_REPORT_WORKFLOW_ID` regardless of `schedule.agentId`.
The field is API-visible and misleading.

### ~~B5-11~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · `calculate-metric` and `db-write` are stubs exposed as working tools
`tools/calculate-metric.tool.ts:19-30`, `tools/db-write.tool.ts:24-42`

`calculate-metric` always returns `{value: 0, unit: 'unknown', breakdown: []}` — but its
tool *description* tells the model it computes real business metrics, so the agent will
present `0` as fact. `db-write` records an audit entry and returns `{accepted: true}`
without writing anything, after burning a human approval. Both are documented as v1 stubs
internally; the model and the end user are not told. At minimum the descriptions should say
"not yet implemented".

### ~~B5-12~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · Unused parameter flagged by lint
`tools/calculate-metric.tool.ts:19` — `'_deps' is defined but never used` (ESLint error).

### ~~B5-13~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · `ChatController` has no `@Roles`, so `agent`-role service credentials can chat
`controllers/chat.controller.ts`

Combined with C-2 below this is a 500, not an escalation — but the intent is unclear.

## B6. system

Cleanest module in the repo. Encryption envelope with version prefix and AES-256-GCM,
`hasSecret` booleans instead of secrets in responses, transactional single-active
enforcement backed by a matching partial unique index, redacted audit metadata (field
*names* only). Only minor notes:

### ~~B6-1~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · Audit writes are best-effort and silently swallowed
`system-audit.service.ts` `record()`

`catch → logger.error` and return. For an audit trail this means a mutation can succeed
with no record of who made it. Acceptable if intentional, but it should be a *stated*
policy — and for secret mutations (SMTP/IMAP/integration credentials) the safer default is
to fail the mutation rather than lose the audit entry.

### ~~B6-2~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `POST /system/{smtp,imap}/:id/test` is an authenticated outbound-connection primitive
`smtp-config.service.ts` / `imap-config.service.ts` `test()`

An admin supplies arbitrary `host`/`port` and the server connects to it, returning the raw
error message. Admin-only, so low severity, but it is an internal-network probe with a
response oracle. Worth a note in the threat model, and worth not returning the raw error.

### ~~B6-3~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · No key rotation path despite a versioned envelope
`encryption.service.ts`

The `v{version}` prefix exists explicitly "to leave room for key rotation", but `decrypt`
ignores the parsed version and always uses the single current key. Rotating
`SYSTEM_ENCRYPTION_KEY` makes every stored secret undecryptable. Either implement a
key-ring keyed by version, or drop the prefix so it does not imply a capability that
is absent.

### ~~B6-4~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · `system_settings` cache is per-process with a 5-minute TTL and no cross-instance invalidation
`system-settings.service.ts:23`

`upsert`/`remove` call `cache.del` on the shared Redis-backed cache-manager, so this is
actually coherent — but the TTL is a magic number in the class rather than config, unlike
the analogous `SEARCH_REGISTRY_TTL_MS`.

## B7. document-ingest

### ~~B7-1~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · Whole documents are buffered in memory, then stored whole in Postgres *and* Meili
`document-extraction.service.ts` (`streamToBuffer`), `document-ingest.processor.ts`

`extract()` buffers the entire file (`FILE_MAX_SIZE` default **50 MB**) into a `Buffer`,
then `pdf-parse`/`mammoth` build a full text string, which is then written verbatim into
`search_records.document` (jsonb) and into a Meili document — with **no truncation or
chunking at any point**. Three compounding problems:

- Memory: worker concurrency × 50 MB buffer × (parsed text + JSON copy). No concurrency
  limit is set on the `document-ingest` queue, so the default applies per replica.
- Postgres: a multi-MB jsonb value per document; `toMeiliDocument` copies it again for
  every re-index and reload.
- Retrieval quality: a whole book as one Meili document is a poor unit for the
  `search-documents` tool the agent uses — the model gets one enormous hit.

The `documents` field spec has no chunking concept at all. This is the main scalability
cliff in the codebase.

### ~~B7-2~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · Extraction failures permanently drop the document with no visible state
`document-ingest.processor.ts` `process()`

A corrupt PDF makes `pdfParse` throw → job retries 3× → job dies (`removeOnFail: 100`).
The `files` row stays `AVAILABLE`, no `search_records` row is ever created, and nothing
records that ingestion failed. The user sees a successfully uploaded file that the agent
can never find. There is no `INGEST_FAILED` state and no reconciliation sweep for
document-ingest (unlike mailbox and search, which both have one).

### ~~B7-3~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · MIME allowlist is empty by default, so ingest MIME and upload MIME disagree
`env.validation.ts` (`FILE_ALLOWED_MIME` default `''` = allow any), `document-ingest.constants.ts`

`isMimeAllowed` returns `true` for everything by default. A file declared
`application/pdf` that is actually a ZIP gets quarantined by magic-byte sniffing (good),
but anything not in the 5-signature table is accepted and handed to `pdf-parse`. Production
should ship a non-empty default allowlist.

### ~~B7-4~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · `title` is never extracted
`document-extraction.service.ts` returns `{ text }` only; `ExtractedDocument.title` is
declared and never populated, so the processor always falls back to `meta.filename`. PDF
metadata and DOCX core properties both carry a title.

### ~~B7-5~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · Bootstrap swallows all errors and never retries
`documents-collection.bootstrap.ts`

`get()` in a `try`, `create()` in a `try` with `logger.warn` on failure. If both fail (DB
slow at boot), the `documents` collection never exists and every subsequent ingest job
fails with `SEARCH_COLLECTION_NOT_FOUND` until someone restarts the process.

## B8. infrastructure — queue

### ~~B8-1~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `ExampleProcessor` / `ExampleScheduler` / `DEFAULT_QUEUE` are dead code in production
`src/infrastructure/queue/`

`QueueModule` is `@Global()` and registers a `default` queue with a live `WorkerHost` that
logs and does nothing, plus a scheduler whose only method is never called. Every replica
runs a BullMQ worker polling an empty queue forever. Delete, or replace with the real
default queue.

### ~~B8-2~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · No `defaultJobOptions` on the BullMQ root
`queue.module.ts:19-31`

Every producer passes its own `{attempts, backoff, removeOnComplete, removeOnFail}` today —
which is disciplined but unenforced. One forgotten option set (e.g. `ExampleScheduler`'s
`{repeat, removeOnComplete: true}` with no `removeOnFail`) leaves completed/failed jobs in
Redis forever. Set them once at the root.

### ~~B8-3~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · Redis client is created with default `ioredis` retry behaviour
`cache/redis.provider.ts:20-30`

No `maxRetriesPerRequest`, `enableReadyCheck`, `connectTimeout`, or `retryStrategy`. The
default `maxRetriesPerRequest: 20` means a command against a down Redis rejects only after
20 retries; `enableOfflineQueue` (default on) means commands issued while disconnected
queue up unboundedly. `IndexRegistry` documents having reasoned about exactly this for its
subscriber (`index-registry.ts:96-104`) — the shared client did not get the same treatment.

