# PART C — Module integration issues

### ~~C-1~~ ✅ FIXED · CRITICAL · The search-service is a shared, unscoped read surface for three private writers
`document-ingest → documents`, `mailbox → inbound_email`, `mastra ← both`

This is the integration form of B1-1/B5-3 and deserves its own entry because no single
module is at fault. Each writer correctly persists per-user data with an owner marker
(`documents.ownerUserId`) or admin-only intent (`inbound_email`). The search-service, by
design, exposes *all* collections through one endpoint with one policy ("reads are global").
The result is that adding a writer silently widens the read audience of its data, and
nothing in either module's tests or types signals it.

The boundary needs an explicit contract: a collection declares its visibility at creation,
and `SearchRecordService` enforces it on `search()`, `get()`, and every agent tool path.

### ~~C-2~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · Service-credential (`agent`) principals violate FK constraints on the agent tables
`auth/service-credential.service.ts` (`sub: cred.id`) → `mastra/services/conversation.service.ts` (`ownerUserId: principal.id`)

`POST /auth/service-token` mints a token whose `sub` is a `service_credentials.id` with
`role: 'agent'`. `ChatController` has no `@Roles`, so that token can call `POST /agent/chat`.
`ConversationService.ensure` then inserts `agentConversations.ownerUserId = <credential id>`,
but that column is `uuid ... references(() => users.id)` (`agent.schema.ts`). Postgres
rejects it (SQLSTATE 23503) → the infra mapper does not recognise 23503 (only 23505) →
generic 500.

The same mismatch applies to `agentRuns.triggeredByUserId`, `agentApprovals.decidedByUserId`,
and `systemAuditLog.actorId`. Meanwhile `files.ownerId` has **no** FK, so an agent-owned
file is silently accepted — inconsistent handling of the same identity problem.

**Fix:** make `Principal` carry its `kind` (`user | service`) and either reject service
principals on user-scoped routes, or add a `service_credentials`-aware actor model.

### ~~C-3~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · `HealthModule` depends on a feature module, coupling readiness to search
`infrastructure/health/health.module.ts` → `features/search-service`

The direction is documented and `@Optional()`-guarded, and the rationale (a live Meili
serving a stale read model is a real outage) is sound. But the consequence is not
mitigated: `MeiliHealthIndicator.isHealthy` calls `status.stats()`, which runs a full
aggregate over `search_records` (`count(*) filter (...)` ×3 plus `min`/`max`) **on every
health probe**. On a k8s readiness probe at 10s intervals across N replicas, that is a
constant sequential-scan-shaped load on the largest table in the schema, and a slow
Postgres will fail the *Meili* indicator. Cache the stats (30–60s) or move lag reporting
off the liveness path.

### ~~C-4~~ ✅ FIXED · HIGH · Nothing runs database migrations, and Mastra's schema is created out-of-band
`package.json` (`db:migrate` is CLI-only), `mastra/memory/memory.factory.ts`

The app never applies migrations at boot (reasonable), but there is also no documented
deploy step that does, and no schema-version assertion — a replica booting against an
un-migrated database fails at first query with raw Postgres errors. Separately, the
`mastra` schema and its tables are created lazily by `@mastra/pg` on first use, so the
database has two independent, uncoordinated schema owners on the same connection pool.
See B5-7 for the concrete failure.

### ~~C-5~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · Every replica runs every worker and every scheduler
All `*.scheduler.ts` (`OnApplicationBootstrap`) + all `@Processor(...)` classes

There is no leader election or worker/API split. Scaling the HTTP tier to N replicas
multiplies: N search-indexing workers (×4 concurrency), N file-processing workers, N
mailbox-sync workers, N agent-run workers, and N sets of `upsertJobScheduler` calls.
The scheduler registrations are idempotent (correctly, via stable ids), so cron does not
duplicate — but the worker fan-out is unbounded and shares the 10-connection pg pool (A-7).
Needs a `WORKER_ENABLED` / `API_ONLY` split before horizontal scaling.

### ~~C-6~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `document-ingest` is triggered only from `file-processing`, with no back-pressure or dedup
`file-processing.processor.ts` → `INGEST_DOCUMENT_QUEUE`

Every `AVAILABLE` ingestable file enqueues an ingest job with no job id, so a re-uploaded
identical file (which `tryDedup` turns into a *new* `files` row pointing at the same object)
enqueues a *second* ingest of the same bytes. `persist` dedupes downstream by
`externalId: fileId` — but the `fileId` differs per row, so the same document lands in the
`documents` collection once per dedup row, each owned by a different user. Combined with
B7-1 that is a full re-parse and a duplicate multi-MB record per upload.

### ~~C-7~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `DocumentsCollectionBootstrap` and `MailboxService` race `CollectionService` at boot
`documents-collection.bootstrap.ts`, `mailbox.service.ts:55`, `collection.service.ts` `onApplicationBootstrap`

Three `OnApplicationBootstrap` hooks touch the collection registry with no defined ordering:
`CollectionService.onApplicationBootstrap` warms the registry and ensures indexes, while the
other two create collections. If a creator runs first, the warm pass may miss it; if the
warm pass runs first, the creators' `registry.invalidate` covers it. It converges via TTL,
but the ordering is accidental rather than designed.

### ~~C-8~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · The mailbox↔search integration silently drops indexing failures
`mailbox-ingest.service.ts` `persist()` (final `try/catch` → `logger.warn`)

Correct at the record level — the row is durable and the 6-hourly reconcile sweep re-drives
it — but `reconcile()` only looks back `RECONCILE_LOOKBACK_MS` (7 days). A message whose
`persist` failed and stays failed for 8 days is never re-attempted by the mailbox sweep;
it is only recoverable if a `search_records` row was actually written (which it was not,
since `persist` threw before the insert on a validation error). Validation failures are
therefore permanent silent data loss from the search index.

### ~~C-9~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · `EmailModule` reads `system`-owned tables directly rather than through `system`
`infrastructure/email/email-config.repository.ts`

Deliberate and documented ("so the email module never imports `system`"), and it does avoid
a cycle. The cost is two owners of `smtp_configs`/`imap_configs`: a schema change to those
tables must be made in two places, and the email side has no visibility into the `isActive`
transaction semantics the system module enforces. Acceptable, but worth an explicit shared
read-model type rather than duplicated row mapping.

### ~~C-10~~ ✅ FIXED · MEDIUM · SMTP/IMAP connections are created per operation, never pooled
`smtp.transport.ts` `buildTransport` (per `sendMail`/`verify`), `imap.transport.ts` `withClient`

Nodemailer supports `pool: true`; ImapFlow supports long-lived sessions and IDLE. See B4-1
for the severe IMAP case; the SMTP case is milder (one connection per email) but the
password-reset flow sends on every request.

### ~~C-11~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · `FileProcessorModule` producer-registers `INGEST_DOCUMENT_QUEUE` to avoid importing `DocumentIngestModule`
`file-processor.module.ts:31`, while `DocumentIngestModule` imports `FileProcessorModule`

Correct technique for breaking the cycle, and documented. But the queue name is now declared
in two modules and the coupling is implicit — a rename in `document-ingest.constants.ts`
compiles fine and silently splits producer from consumer. A shared queue-registry constant
module would make the contract explicit.

### ~~C-12~~ ✅ FIXED · LOW · `SearchServiceModule` is imported by five modules
`app.module`, `health.module`, `mailbox.module`, `document-ingest.module`, `mastra.module`

Nest singletons make this correct, but it means the search feature is effectively
infrastructure. If that is the intent, its stable services (`SearchRecordService`) belong
behind an interface token like `SEARCH_ENGINE` is, so the four consumers depend on a port
rather than a concrete feature service.

### C-13 ⏸ DEFERRED · LOW · Mastra routes are mounted at `/api/agent-core` while everything else is at root
`mastra.module.ts` (`prefix: '/api/agent-core'`)

See A-6. Also, the adapter's catch-all controller is an undocumented public surface — it
is worth verifying which of its routes are reachable and whether the global `JwtAuthGuard`
covers them (they are registered as Nest controllers, so they should be, but there is no
test asserting it).

